1479|fn2:q1|prose|Footnote 2|1||||"it should be pointed out that the present-day ḍoms do not represent a single caste or a homogenous group. In fact, the modern reflexes of the word ḍomba- seem rather to be cover terms for a number of castes which may share certain features, such as being ""low caste"" and having a similar socio-cultural and economic profile. However, various ḍom groups in different parts of India may not share a common origin"| ( Beníšek 2009:349).|"See Beníšek 2009 for an historical account of the term ḍom/ḍomba- in India. According to him, “it should be pointed out that the present-day ḍoms do not represent a single caste or a homogenous group. In fact, the modern reflexes of the word ḍomba- seem rather to be cover terms for a number of castes which may share certain features, such as being ""low caste"" and having a similar socio-cultural and economic profile. However, various ḍom groups in different parts of India may not share a common origin.” ( Beníšek 2009:349)"
1480|fn4:q1|prose|Footnote 4|1||||The linguistic affinity between Romani and Domari (and, as far as documented, Lomavren) might therefore be accounted for in terms of their shared ancient origin and subsequent similar social and geographical history, rather than as a token of continuous genetic ties in the form of a linguistic sub-branch within the Indo-Aryan languages| (Matras 2002:48).|Matras sums up the situation saying that “The linguistic affinity between Romani and Domari (and, as far as documented, Lomavren) might therefore be accounted for in terms of their shared ancient origin and subsequent similar social and geographical history, rather than as a token of continuous genetic ties in the form of a linguistic sub-branch within the Indo-Aryan languages.” (Matras 2002:48)
